A code I genuinely cannot decode

Here’s a strong boundary.

Give me a truly random bitstring:

101110001001...

generated as a one-time-pad ciphertext, with a uniformly random key used exactly once.

Without the key or external information, there isn’t a clever enough semantic trick that recovers the plaintext.

For every candidate plaintext P, there exists some key:

K=C\oplus P

that makes it fit.

So:

P(P_i\mid C)=P(P_j\mid C)

under the ideal assumptions.

There is no footprint to follow.

That’s useful for Pantheon philosophically:

Delta requires asymmetry.

If every interpretation fits equally well, inference has no gradient.

No wave.

Nothing for the surfer to catch.

